*****STEP ONE Option A: PAIR YOUR READS AND IMPORT DATA INTO QIIME*****************************


***STEP ONE OPTION A: Without Manifest File

I honestly think you should be able to get away with not creating a manifest file... Josh used to import his data from University of Buffalo to QIIME using the following because of how the sample files are labeled when we get them back from University of Buffalo, but if you're worried about the pairs getting mapped to one another correctly, or are having issues with that, use the manifest method instead.

qiime tools import \
  --type "SampleData[PairedEndSequencesWithQuality]" \
  --input-format CasavaOneEightLanelessPerSampleDirFmt \
  --input-path raw_copy\
  --output-path demux-paired-end.qza


***STEP ONE OPTION B: With Manifest File

- Since our data is demultiplexed, it is suggested that we use a fastq manifest file. "A fastq manifest file is a type of sample metadata file that maps sample identifiers to one or two absolute filepaths pointing at .fastq.gz (or .fastq) files, depending on whether you’re importing data from a single- or paried-end run."

The following is an example of how that file should look for paired-end reads.

File name: fq-manifest.tsv

	sample-id forward-absolute-filepath   reverse-absolute-filepath
	sample-1  /scratch/microbiome/sample1_R1.fastq.gz  /scratch/microbiome/sample1_R2.fastq.gz
	sample-2  /scratch/microbiome/sample2_R1.fastq.gz  /scratch/microbiome/sample2_R2.fastq.gz

Once you've constructed the fq-manifest.tsv, run the following command:

qiime tools import \
 --type 'SampleData[PairedEndSequencesWithQuality]' \
 --input-path fq-manifest.tsv \
 --output-path demux-paired-end.qza \
 --input-format PairedEndFastqManifestPhred33V2

#*********************************************************************************************
COMPARISON NOTES:

"In QIIME 2, both CasavaOneEightLanelessPerSampleDirFmt and PairedEndFastqManifestPhred33V2 are formats used to import demultiplexed, paired-end FASTQ files. The core difference lies in whether the input files follow strict Illumina Casava naming conventions or if you are defining their location manually via a manifest file. 


1. CasavaOneEightLanelessPerSampleDirFmt
This format is designed for data demultiplexed by Illumina’s CASAVA (or bcl2fastq) software. It expects a specific directory structure and file naming convention where QIIME 2 parses the sample IDs, lane numbers, and directions directly from the filename. 

Best For: Data straight from an Illumina MiSeq/NextSeq that is already demultiplexed.
Filename Format: sampleid_barcodeid_L001_R1_001.fastq.gz (Matches: .+_.+_L[0-9][0-9][0-9]_R[12]_001\.fastq\.gz).
Requirements:
Files must be in a single directory.
Must be gzipped (.fastq.gz).
Must contain _R1_ and _R2_ in the names.
Pros: No need to create a manifest file.
Cons: Very strict filename requirements; files must be renamed if they don't match exactly. 

2. PairedEndFastqManifestPhred33V2
This format provides maximum flexibility. Instead of relying on file names, you provide a tab-delimited text file (the "manifest") that explicitly maps sample IDs to their corresponding forward and reverse fastq file paths. 

Best For: Data with custom naming conventions, data from multiple sources, or when you want to avoid renaming hundreds of files.
Structure: A file containing: sample-id, forward-absolute-filepath, reverse-absolute-filepath.
Requirements:
Phred33 quality scores (standard for modern Illumina).
Tabs must be used in the manifest (not spaces).
Pros: Flexible; enables importing files from any location, regardless of name.
Cons: Requires manual creation of the manifest file. ""
