"To determine the right alpha rarefaction sample depth, you must balance maximizing your sequencing depth with retaining enough samples. Your chosen depth should fall within the range where the rarefaction curves (such as observed species or Shannon diversity) visibly flatten out (plateau), meaning additional sequences yield minimal new taxonomic discoveries.Reviewing the following key signs helps verify your selection:The Plateau Effect: The curves on your plot should level off horizontally. If your curves are still pointing straight up at your chosen depth, your sample depth is too low, and you are missing a significant portion of the microbial diversity in those samples.Sample Retention: Check how many samples you are forced to discard. If you pick a very high depth, you will capture maximum diversity but might lose critical, low-read samples.

Setting your alpha rarefaction sampling depth too low causes artificial loss of diversity, underrepresented rare taxa, and a false flattening (plateau) of your rarefaction curves. This strips away statistical power, making it difficult to detect true biological differences between your samples.

Inflated False Negatives: By shrinking the number of reads supporting your diversity estimates, you lose the sensitivity to detect actual differences between treatment groups.Loss of Rare Taxa: You will consistently miss low-abundance microbes that only appear in deeper sequencing, skewing the richness and evenness profile of your samples.Misleading Plateaus: Curves can artificially "level out" at a low sampling depth. This suggests you have captured all community diversity, but in reality, you have simply choked the data.Sample Discard Imbalance: While a low threshold may seem like a good way to save shallowly sequenced samples, you end up discarding highly informative, deeply sequenced samples or over-truncating them to the lowest common denominator.

If you set your alpha rarefaction sampling depth too high, you will drop a large number of your samples from the analysis. You risk throwing away valuable data, making your downstream estimates less reliable and potentially biasing your results.

Sample Loss: Rarefying forces the dataset to subsample all samples to a single, even depth. If your chosen depth is higher than the read count of your shallowest samples, those samples will be completely discarded.Decreased Statistical Power: Throwing away samples shrinks your dataset, which reduces your ability to accurately detect true biological differences between your groups.Distorted Diversity Estimates: Subsampling too deeply forces the algorithm to generate artificial estimations on sparse data, which can increase variability and hide true diversity.

Yes, you should use the same sampling depth for both QIIME 2 workflows. Both qiime diversity alpha-rarefaction and qiime diversity core-metrics-phylogenetic require an even sampling depth to standardize libraries. Keeping this value identical ensures that your diversity comparisons are statistically valid and directly comparable across the board.

